WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:09.820
In this module, we're going to take a closer look at the components of the Unix system.

2
00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:15.820
Let's start with a bit of background into how Unix was written.

3
00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:21.820
C was developed to make Unix independent of hardware architecture.

4
00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:28.820
When the Unix kernel and shell were written in C, it made programming and debugging easier.

5
00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:32.820
C was developed by programmers for programmers.

6
00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:36.820
C compilers were made available for different Unix versions.

7
00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:43.820
In the late 1970s, the popularity of C grew and C compilers became available for a large number of processes

8
00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:46.820
and for a large number of operating systems.

9
00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:54.820
C has a rich set of functions that deal with all aspects of data manipulation, I.O., math, and character functions.

10
00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:58.820
The ability of C to use more traditional high-level constructs

11
00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:04.820
gives the programmer elegance and flexibility while maintaining the options for raw power.

12
00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:12.820
C++ is an object-oriented version of C that has become popular with object-oriented design and programming.

13
00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:18.820
A Unix system is usually represented as a set of circles with functions in layers.

14
00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:23.820
The first layer is the hardware, which interfaces directly with the kernel.

15
00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:28.820
The kernel is the base operating system which provides services for other layers.

16
00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:35.820
Built on the kernel is the command interface, often called the shell, and the commands and the utilities.

17
00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:40.820
There are a number of different shells available for Unix systems.

18
00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:47.820
The last layer is the application programs, which are built on the application programming interfaces,

19
00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:51.820
the shell, and the commands and utilities.

20
00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:55.820
The Unix kernel is the base operating system.

21
00:01:55.820 --> 00:02:01.820
Most of the kernel is indeed written in C, which makes it easy to port to other processes.

22
00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:10.820
There are routines that are dependent on the machine architecture for functions like memory mapping and interrupt handling.

23
00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:19.820
The kernel provides basic functions like process scheduling, memory management, device control, and file management.

24
00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:25.820
There's also a system call interface, and there's an operator interface.

25
00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:30.820
Unix is implemented in the layered approach that's shown here.

26
00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:36.820
So at the base, there is the specific manufacturer's hardware.

27
00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:41.820
A unique hardware interface is provided to achieve complete hardware independence.

28
00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:46.820
It's partially written in assembler and partially written in C.

29
00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:51.820
This is the only vendor-specific part of the Unix implementation.

30
00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:56.820
The upper levels of Unix, well, as we know, those are all written in C.

31
00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:59.820
Now, the command interface in a Unix system is called the shell.

32
00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:05.820
Why shell? Because it's the outermost layer of the Unix system.

33
00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:12.820
It serves as a command language interpreter and programming language and allows foreground and background processing.

34
00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:17.820
When you log on to a Unix system, you start to communicate with the shell.

35
00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:21.820
The shell can be compared to TSO in a ZOS system.

36
00:03:21.820 --> 00:03:27.820
Essentially, the shell is a program which allows users to communicate with the operating system.

37
00:03:27.820 --> 00:03:37.820
The shell reads commands that are entered and interprets them as requests to execute other programs, access files, or provide output.

38
00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:40.820
The shell doesn't really have a user-friendly interface.

39
00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:45.820
It's driven by commands, which if you're unfamiliar with them, they can seem a bit cryptic,

40
00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:49.820
but programmers and system programmers like it because of its power.

41
00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:56.820
Today, though, vendors do provide solutions for Unix users to replace the shell interface with a window interface or a menu-driven interface,

42
00:03:56.820 --> 00:04:01.820
which is easier to use for users who aren't already familiar with Unix command syntax.

43
00:04:01.820 --> 00:04:04.820
There are two native command interface shells.

44
00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:10.820
The Born shell is the most basic shell, and it's the default ZOS Unix shell.

45
00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:15.820
The C shell has the functionality of the C language and is preferred by many programmers.

46
00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:22.820
Access the command line of any Unix-like operating system, and there's a good chance you'll find a Bash shell.

47
00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:31.820
Bash has become the default shell on both Linux and macOS, making the ability to work with it an essential skill for pretty much any software developer.

48
00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:40.820
Bash, which stands for, wait for it, Born Again shell, was designed as a replacement for the Born shell

49
00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:43.820
and is available on Z through Rocket Software.

50
00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:49.820
Now, in the next video, we'll look at some fundamental functions of Unix, starting with commands.
